@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.2451 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [8504 x i8] }> <{ [8504 x i8] c"# `coroutines`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: [#43122]\0A\0A[#43122]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/43122\0A\0A------------------------\0A\0AThe `coroutines` feature gate in Rust allows you to define coroutine or\0Acoroutine literals. A coroutine is a \22resumable function\22 that syntactically\0Aresembles a closure but compiles to much different semantics in the compiler\0Aitself. The primary feature of a coroutine is that it can be suspended during\0Aexecution to be resumed at a later date. Coroutines use the `yield` keyword to\0A\22return\22, and then the caller can `resume` a coroutine to resume execution just\0Aafter the `yield` keyword.\0A\0ACoroutines are an extra-unstable feature in the compiler right now. Added in\0A[RFC 2033] they're mostly intended right now as a information/constraint\0Agathering phase. The intent is that experimentation can happen on the nightly\0Acompiler before actual stabilization. A further RFC will be required to\0Astabilize coroutines and will likely contain at least a few small\0Atweaks to the overall design.\0A\0A[RFC 2033]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/2033\0A\0AA syntactical example of a coroutine is:\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(coroutines, coroutine_trait)]\0A\0Ause std::ops::{Coroutine, CoroutineState};\0Ause std::pin::Pin;\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    let mut coroutine = || {\0A        yield 1;\0A        return \22foo\22\0A    };\0A\0A    match Pin::new(&mut coroutine).resume(()) {\0A        CoroutineState::Yielded(1) => {}\0A        _ => panic!(\22unexpected value from resume\22),\0A    }\0A    match Pin::new(&mut coroutine).resume(()) {\0A        CoroutineState::Complete(\22foo\22) => {}\0A        _ => panic!(\22unexpected value from resume\22),\0A    }\0A}\0A```\0A\0ACoroutines are closure-like literals which can contain a `yield` statement. The\0A`yield` statement takes an optional expression of a value to yield out of the\0Acoroutine. All coroutine literals implement the `Coroutine` trait in the\0A`std::ops` module. The `Coroutine` trait has one main method, `resume`, which\0Aresumes execution of the coroutine at the previous suspension point.\0A\0AAn example of the control flow of coroutines is that the following example\0Aprints all numbers in order:\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(coroutines, coroutine_trait)]\0A\0Ause std::ops::Coroutine;\0Ause std::pin::Pin;\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    let mut coroutine = || {\0A        println!(\222\22);\0A        yield;\0A        println!(\224\22);\0A    };\0A\0A    println!(\221\22);\0A    Pin::new(&mut coroutine).resume(());\0A    println!(\223\22);\0A    Pin::new(&mut coroutine).resume(());\0A    println!(\225\22);\0A}\0A```\0A\0AAt this time the main intended use case of coroutines is an implementation\0Aprimitive for async/await syntax, but coroutines will likely be extended to\0Aergonomic implementations of iterators and other primitives in the future.\0AFeedback on the design and usage is always appreciated!\0A\0A### The `Coroutine` trait\0A\0AThe `Coroutine` trait in `std::ops` currently looks like:\0A\0A```rust\0A# #![feature(arbitrary_self_types, coroutine_trait)]\0A# use std::ops::CoroutineState;\0A# use std::pin::Pin;\0A\0Apub trait Coroutine<R = ()> {\0A    type Yield;\0A    type Return;\0A    fn resume(self: Pin<&mut Self>, resume: R) -> CoroutineState<Self::Yield, Self::Return>;\0A}\0A```\0A\0AThe `Coroutine::Yield` type is the type of values that can be yielded with the\0A`yield` statement. The `Coroutine::Return` type is the returned type of the\0Acoroutine. This is typically the last expression in a coroutine's definition or\0Aany value passed to `return` in a coroutine. The `resume` function is the entry\0Apoint for executing the `Coroutine` itself.\0A\0AThe return value of `resume`, `CoroutineState`, looks like:\0A\0A```rust\0Apub enum CoroutineState<Y, R> {\0A    Yielded(Y),\0A    Complete(R),\0A}\0A```\0A\0AThe `Yielded` variant indicates that the coroutine can later be resumed. This\0Acorresponds to a `yield` point in a coroutine. The `Complete` variant indicates\0Athat the coroutine is complete and cannot be resumed again. Calling `resume`\0Aafter a coroutine has returned `Complete` will likely result in a panic of the\0Aprogram.\0A\0A### Closure-like semantics\0A\0AThe closure-like syntax for coroutines alludes to the fact that they also have\0Aclosure-like semantics. Namely:\0A\0A* When created, a coroutine executes no code. A closure literal does not\0A  actually execute any of the closure's code on construction, and similarly a\0A  coroutine literal does not execute any code inside the coroutine when\0A  constructed.\0A\0A* Coroutines can capture outer variables by reference or by move, and this can\0A  be tweaked with the `move` keyword at the beginning of the closure. Like\0A  closures all coroutines will have an implicit environment which is inferred by\0A  the compiler. Outer variables can be moved into a coroutine for use as the\0A  coroutine progresses.\0A\0A* Coroutine literals produce a value with a unique type which implements the\0A  `std::ops::Coroutine` trait. This allows actual execution of the coroutine\0A  through the `Coroutine::resume` method as well as also naming it in return\0A  types and such.\0A\0A* Traits like `Send` and `Sync` are automatically implemented for a `Coroutine`\0A  depending on the captured variables of the environment. Unlike closures,\0A  coroutines also depend on variables live across suspension points. This means\0A  that although the ambient environment may be `Send` or `Sync`, the coroutine\0A  itself may not be due to internal variables live across `yield` points being\0A  not-`Send` or not-`Sync`. Note that coroutines do\0A  not implement traits like `Copy` or `Clone` automatically.\0A\0A* Whenever a coroutine is dropped it will drop all captured environment\0A  variables.\0A\0A### Coroutines as state machines\0A\0AIn the compiler, coroutines are currently compiled as state machines. Each\0A`yield` expression will correspond to a different state that stores all live\0Avariables over that suspension point. Resumption of a coroutine will dispatch on\0Athe current state and then execute internally until a `yield` is reached, at\0Awhich point all state is saved off in the coroutine and a value is returned.\0A\0ALet's take a look at an example to see what's going on here:\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(coroutines, coroutine_trait)]\0A\0Ause std::ops::Coroutine;\0Ause std::pin::Pin;\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    let ret = \22foo\22;\0A    let mut coroutine = move || {\0A        yield 1;\0A        return ret\0A    };\0A\0A    Pin::new(&mut coroutine).resume(());\0A    Pin::new(&mut coroutine).resume(());\0A}\0A```\0A\0AThis coroutine literal will compile down to something similar to:\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(arbitrary_self_types, coroutines, coroutine_trait)]\0A\0Ause std::ops::{Coroutine, CoroutineState};\0Ause std::pin::Pin;\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    let ret = \22foo\22;\0A    let mut coroutine = {\0A        enum __Coroutine {\0A            Start(&'static str),\0A            Yield1(&'static str),\0A            Done,\0A        }\0A\0A        impl Coroutine for __Coroutine {\0A            type Yield = i32;\0A            type Return = &'static str;\0A\0A            fn resume(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, resume: ()) -> CoroutineState<i32, &'static str> {\0A                use std::mem;\0A                match mem::replace(&mut *self, __Coroutine::Done) {\0A                    __Coroutine::Start(s) => {\0A                        *self = __Coroutine::Yield1(s);\0A                        CoroutineState::Yielded(1)\0A                    }\0A\0A                    __Coroutine::Yield1(s) => {\0A                        *self = __Coroutine::Done;\0A                        CoroutineState::Complete(s)\0A                    }\0A\0A                    __Coroutine::Done => {\0A                        panic!(\22coroutine resumed after completion\22)\0A                    }\0A                }\0A            }\0A        }\0A\0A        __Coroutine::Start(ret)\0A    };\0A\0A    Pin::new(&mut coroutine).resume(());\0A    Pin::new(&mut coroutine).resume(());\0A}\0A```\0A\0ANotably here we can see that the compiler is generating a fresh type,\0A`__Coroutine` in this case. This type has a number of states (represented here\0Aas an `enum`) corresponding to each of the conceptual states of the coroutine.\0AAt the beginning we're closing over our outer variable `foo` and then that\0Avariable is also live over the `yield` point, so it's stored in both states.\0A\0AWhen the coroutine starts it'll immediately yield 1, but it saves off its state\0Ajust before it does so indicating that it has reached the yield point. Upon\0Aresuming again we'll execute the `return ret` which returns the `Complete`\0Astate.\0A\0AHere we can also note that the `Done` state, if resumed, panics immediately as\0Ait's invalid to resume a completed coroutine. It's also worth noting that this\0Ais just a rough desugaring, not a normative specification for what the compiler\0Adoes.\0A" }>, align 1
